  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nNot a lot of changes to the current forecast. Still expect clouds \nwill increase ahead of an approaching frontal system this evening. \nThe strong system will move across the midwest and approach the\nOhio Valley by early Thursday morning. Expect showers and \nthunderstorms enter into area as a healthy shortwave moves \nacross the region. Some of these storms could be strong to \nsevere. SPC outlooks continue to focus on a wind threat. Given \nstrong 850MB winds, this is a definite concern. Additionally, \nheavy rain could lead localized flooding as precipitable water \nvalues are very high with amounts in excess of 2 inches in some\nareas.\n\n\nExpect storm chances to continue into the afternoon Thursday, \nespecially in Eastern KY, central and southern WV. Strong to \nsevere weather is possible at this time. The environmental \nparameters are impressive with strong 850 low level jet and deep\nlayer flow. Rapid development of afternoon storms with adequate\nheating could result in another round of damaging winds. The \npotential for a brief tornado cannot be ruled out. Excessive \nrainfall from any storm could result in some localized flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe weekend will start on the drier side but another area of low \npressure will move toward the Tri-State by Sunday. As a result, the \nthreat for showers and thunderstorms return. Should the timing of \nthe front slow, this activity could continue to start the workweek. \n\nHigh pressure will allow the area to dry out behind the exiting late \nweekend system.",
